      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>You can install a cluster on RHOSP infrastructure that you provision. By using this installation method, you can integrate your cluster with existing infrastructure and modifications. For installations on user-provisioned infrastructure, you must create all RHOSP resources, like Nova servers, Neutron ports, and security groups. You can use the provided Ansible playbooks to assist with the deployment process.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      <h2 id="security-osp-validating-certificates_preparing-to-install-on-openstack" class="group scroll-mt-20">Scanning RHOSP endpoints for legacy HTTPS certificates<a href="#security-osp-validating-certificates_preparing-to-install-on-openstack" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Beginning with OpenShift Container Platform 4.10, HTTPS certificates must contain subject alternative name (SAN) fields. You can run a script to scan each HTTPS endpoint in a Red Hat OpenStack Platform (RHOSP) catalog for legacy certificates that only contain the <code>CommonName</code> field.</p>
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>OpenShift Container Platform does not check the underlying RHOSP infrastructure for legacy certificates before installation or updates. Use the provided script to check for these certificates yourself. Failing to update legacy certificates before installing or updating a cluster might result in issues for your cluster.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      On the machine where you run the script, have the following software:
      <ul>
      <li>Bash version 4.0 or greater</li>
      <li><code>grep</code></li>
      <li><a href="https://access.redhat.com/documentation/en-us/red_hat_openstack_platform/16.2/html/command_line_interface_reference/the_openstack_client">OpenStack client</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://stedolan.github.io/jq/"><code>jq</code></a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.openssl.org/">OpenSSL version 1.1.1l or greater</a></li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>Populate the machine with RHOSP credentials for the target cloud.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Save the following script to your machine:
      <CodeBlock code={"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n\nset -Eeuo pipefail\n\ndeclare catalog san\ncatalog=\"$(mktemp)\"\nsan=\"$(mktemp)\"\nreadonly catalog san\n\ndeclare invalid=0\n\nopenstack catalog list --format json --column Name --column Endpoints \\\n\t| jq -r '.[] | .Name as $name | .Endpoints[] | select(.interface==\"public\") | [$name, .interface, .url] | join(\" \")' \\\n\t| sort \\\n\t> \"$catalog\"\n\nwhile read -r name interface url; do\n\t# Ignore HTTP\n\tif [[ ${url#\"http://\"} != \"$url\" ]]; then\n\t\tcontinue\n\tfi\n\n\t# Remove the schema from the URL\n\tnoschema=${url#\"https://\"}\n\n\t# If the schema was not HTTPS, error\n\tif [[ \"$noschema\" == \"$url\" ]]; then\n\t\techo \"ERROR (unknown schema): $name $interface $url\"\n\t\texit 2\n\tfi\n\n\t# Remove the path and only keep host and port\n\tnoschema=\"${noschema%%/*}\"\n\thost=\"${noschema%%:*}\"\n\tport=\"${noschema##*:}\"\n\n\t# Add the port if was implicit\n\tif [[ \"$port\" == \"$host\" ]]; then\n\t\tport='443'\n\tfi\n\n\t# Get the SAN fields\n\topenssl s_client -showcerts -servername \"$host\" -connect \"$host:$port\" </dev/null 2>/dev/null \\\n\t\t| openssl x509 -noout -ext subjectAltName \\\n\t\t> \"$san\"\n\n\t# openssl returns the empty string if no SAN is found.\n\t# If a SAN is found, openssl is expected to return something like:\n\t#\n\t#    X509v3 Subject Alternative Name:\n\t#        DNS:standalone, DNS:osp1, IP Address:192.168.2.1, IP Address:10.254.1.2\n\tif [[ \"$(grep -c \"Subject Alternative Name\" \"$san\" || true)\" -gt 0 ]]; then\n\t\techo \"PASS: $name $interface $url\"\n\telse\n\t\tinvalid=$((invalid+1))\n\t\techo \"INVALID: $name $interface $url\"\n\tfi\ndone < \"$catalog\"\n\n# clean up temporary files\nrm \"$catalog\" \"$san\"\n\nif [[ $invalid -gt 0 ]]; then\n\techo \"${invalid} legacy certificates were detected. Update your certificates to include a SAN field.\"\n\texit 1\nelse\n\techo \"All HTTPS certificates for this cloud are valid.\"\nfi"} lang="bash" />
      </li>
      <li>Run the script.</li>
      <li>
      Replace any certificates that the script reports as <code>INVALID</code> with certificates that contain SAN fields.
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>You must replace all legacy HTTPS certificates before you install OpenShift Container Platform 4.10 or update a cluster to that version. Legacy certificates will be rejected with the following message:</p>
          <CodeBlock code={"x509: certificate relies on legacy Common Name field, use SANs instead"} lang="txt" />
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h3 id="security-osp-validating-certificates-manually_preparing-to-install-on-openstack" class="group scroll-mt-20">Scanning RHOSP endpoints for legacy HTTPS certificates manually<a href="#security-osp-validating-certificates-manually_preparing-to-install-on-openstack" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p class="_abstract">Starting in OpenShift Container Platform 4.10, HTTPS certificates require subject alternative name (SAN) fields. If you do not have access to the prerequisite tools that are listed in "Scanning RHOSP endpoints for legacy HTTPS certificates", you can perform certain steps. </p>
      <p>These steps scan each HTTPS endpoint in a Red Hat OpenStack Platform (RHOSP) catalog for legacy certificates that only contain the <code>CommonName</code> field.</p>
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>OpenShift Container Platform does not check the underlying RHOSP infrastructure for legacy certificates before installation or updates. Use the procedure steps to check for these certificates yourself. Failing to update legacy certificates before installing or updating a cluster might result in issues for your cluster.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      On a command line, run the following command to view the URL of RHOSP public endpoints:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ openstack catalog list"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Record the URL for each HTTPS endpoint that the command returns.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      For each public endpoint, note the host and the port.
      <Alert variant="tip" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Tip</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>Determine the host of an endpoint by removing the scheme, the port, and the path.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      For each endpoint, run the following commands to extract the SAN field of the certificate:
      <ol>
      <li>
      Set a <code>host</code> variable:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ host=<host_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Set a <code>port</code> variable:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ port=<port_number>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>If the URL of the endpoint does not have a port, use the value <code>443</code>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Retrieve the SAN field of the certificate:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ openssl s_client -showcerts -servername \"$host\" -connect \"$host:$port\" </dev/null 2>/dev/null \\\n    | openssl x509 -noout -ext subjectAltName"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"X509v3 Subject Alternative Name:\n    DNS:your.host.example.net"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <p>For each endpoint, look for output that resembles the previous example. If there is no output for an endpoint, the certificate of that endpoint is invalid and must be re-issued.</p>
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>You must replace all legacy HTTPS certificates before you install OpenShift Container Platform 4.10 or update a cluster to that version. Legacy certificates are rejected with the following message:</p>
          <CodeBlock code={"x509: certificate relies on legacy Common Name field, use SANs instead"} lang="txt" />
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      </ol>
